{"content_hash": "3ea540f88411c502", "version_id": 233, "article_id": 24, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Plague Never Went Away: Cases Still Appear in Africa and the US", "title_zh": "鼠疫其实从未消失，非洲和美国每年仍有病例", "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-plague-cases-africa-year.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://scx1.b-cdn.net/csz/news/tmb/2026/the-plague-never-went.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 286, "reading_minutes": 1.4, "lead": "Many people think the plague ended hundreds of years ago. However, it is still with us today, and doctors can now treat it with antibiotics.", "lead_zh": "很多人以为鼠疫几百年前就结束了。不过，它其实一直存在，而且现在医生可以用抗生素治疗它。", "preview": "The plague is a rare bacterial infection that once killed tens of millions of people in Europe. Although it is famous for the Black Death in", "preview_zh": "鼠疫是一种罕见的细菌感染，曾在欧洲导致数千万人死亡。虽然它因14世纪的黑死病而出名，但它从未消失。根据世界卫生组织的数据，自20世纪90年代以来，大多数人类病例都出现在非洲，尤其是刚果和马达加斯加。在美国，每年大约有7个病例，主要集中在美国西部的农村地区。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 223, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@17fa5ff/content/audio/24/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 107.025, "size_bytes": 645840, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 334, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@17fa5ff/content/data/versions/233.json", "paragraphs": ["The plague is a rare bacterial infection that once killed tens of millions of people in Europe. Although it is famous for the Black Death in the 14th century, it never went away. Since the 1990s, most human cases have been reported in Africa, especially in Congo and Madagascar, according to the World Health Organization. In the US, there are about seven cases each year, mostly in rural areas of the American West.", "The disease is caused by bacteria called Yersinia pestis, which spread naturally among rodents like rats and prairie dogs. Fleas that bite infected animals can then bite people and pass on the disease. The most common form is bubonic plague, which causes painful swollen lymph nodes, high fever, and chills. If it is not treated, it can develop into two other dangerous forms: pneumonic plague in the lungs and septicemic plague in the blood.", "People can also get plague by touching infected bodily fluids, so experts say you should be careful when handling sick or dead animals. Pneumonic plague can spread through coughing, but only if you are very close to the person. \"If you cough, you can spread it to another person, but that doesn't travel very far,\" said Dr. Glenn Wortmann. Recently, a worker at a plague research institute in Siberia died of pneumonia, and the institute was quarantined.", "The good news is that plague can be cured with antibiotics if you see a doctor early. There is a vaccine, but the WHO only recommends it for high-risk people like lab workers. There is no plague vaccine in the US. As Simon Clarke from the University of Reading said, \"People think the plague is gone... It's not. It's here amongst us.\""], "paragraphs_zh": ["鼠疫是一种罕见的细菌感染，曾在欧洲导致数千万人死亡。虽然它因14世纪的黑死病而出名，但它从未消失。根据世界卫生组织的数据，自20世纪90年代以来，大多数人类病例都出现在非洲，尤其是刚果和马达加斯加。在美国，每年大约有7个病例，主要集中在美国西部的农村地区。", "这种疾病由一种叫鼠疫耶尔森菌的细菌引起，这种细菌在老鼠和草原犬鼠等啮齿动物中自然传播。跳蚤咬了受感染的动物后再咬人，就可能把疾病传给人。最常见的形式是腺鼠疫，它会导致淋巴结肿痛、高烧和发冷。如果不治疗，它可能发展成另外两种危险形式：肺鼠疫和败血型鼠疫。", "人们也可能通过接触受感染的体液而感染鼠疫，所以专家建议处理生病或死亡的动物时要小心。肺鼠疫可以通过咳嗽传播，但只有非常近距离接触才会传染。格伦·沃特曼医生说：“如果你咳嗽，你可以传染给别人，但传播距离不会很远。”最近，西伯利亚一家鼠疫研究所的一名工作人员死于肺炎，该研究所已被隔离。", "好消息是，如果及早就医，鼠疫可以用抗生素治愈。有一种疫苗，但世卫组织只建议实验室工作人员等高危人群接种。美国没有鼠疫疫苗。正如雷丁大学的西蒙·克拉克所说：“人们以为鼠疫消失了……其实没有。它就在我们身边。”"], "body": "The plague is a rare bacterial infection that once killed tens of millions of people in Europe. Although it is famous for the Black Death in the 14th century, it never went away. Since the 1990s, most human cases have been reported in Africa, especially in Congo and Madagascar, according to the World Health Organization. In the US, there are about seven cases each year, mostly in rural areas of the American West.\n\nThe disease is caused by bacteria called Yersinia pestis, which spread naturally among rodents like rats and prairie dogs. Fleas that bite infected animals can then bite people and pass on the disease. The most common form is bubonic plague, which causes painful swollen lymph nodes, high fever, and chills. If it is not treated, it can develop into two other dangerous forms: pneumonic plague in the lungs and septicemic plague in the blood.\n\nPeople can also get plague by touching infected bodily fluids, so experts say you should be careful when handling sick or dead animals. Pneumonic plague can spread through coughing, but only if you are very close to the person. \"If you cough, you can spread it to another person, but that doesn't travel very far,\" said Dr. Glenn Wortmann. Recently, a worker at a plague research institute in Siberia died of pneumonia, and the institute was quarantined.\n\nThe good news is that plague can be cured with antibiotics if you see a doctor early. There is a vaccine, but the WHO only recommends it for high-risk people like lab workers. There is no plague vaccine in the US. As Simon Clarke from the University of Reading said, \"People think the plague is gone... It's not. It's here amongst us.\"", "body_zh": "鼠疫是一种罕见的细菌感染，曾在欧洲导致数千万人死亡。虽然它因14世纪的黑死病而出名，但它从未消失。根据世界卫生组织的数据，自20世纪90年代以来，大多数人类病例都出现在非洲，尤其是刚果和马达加斯加。在美国，每年大约有7个病例，主要集中在美国西部的农村地区。\n\n这种疾病由一种叫鼠疫耶尔森菌的细菌引起，这种细菌在老鼠和草原犬鼠等啮齿动物中自然传播。跳蚤咬了受感染的动物后再咬人，就可能把疾病传给人。最常见的形式是腺鼠疫，它会导致淋巴结肿痛、高烧和发冷。如果不治疗，它可能发展成另外两种危险形式：肺鼠疫和败血型鼠疫。\n\n人们也可能通过接触受感染的体液而感染鼠疫，所以专家建议处理生病或死亡的动物时要小心。肺鼠疫可以通过咳嗽传播，但只有非常近距离接触才会传染。格伦·沃特曼医生说：“如果你咳嗽，你可以传染给别人，但传播距离不会很远。”最近，西伯利亚一家鼠疫研究所的一名工作人员死于肺炎，该研究所已被隔离。\n\n好消息是，如果及早就医，鼠疫可以用抗生素治愈。有一种疫苗，但世卫组织只建议实验室工作人员等高危人群接种。美国没有鼠疫疫苗。正如雷丁大学的西蒙·克拉克所说：“人们以为鼠疫消失了……其实没有。它就在我们身边。”", "text": "The plague is a rare bacterial infection that once killed tens of millions of people in Europe. Although it is famous for the Black Death in the 14th century, it never went away. Since the 1990s, most human cases have been reported in Africa, especially in Congo and Madagascar, according to the World Health Organization. In the US, there are about seven cases each year, mostly in rural areas of the American West. The disease is caused by bacteria called Yersinia pestis, which spread naturally among rodents like rats and prairie dogs. Fleas that bite infected animals can then bite people and pass on the disease. The most common form is bubonic plague, which causes painful swollen lymph nodes, high fever, and chills. If it is not treated, it can develop into two other dangerous forms: pneumonic plague in the lungs and septicemic plague in the blood. People can also get plague by touching infected bodily fluids, so experts say you should be careful when handling sick or dead animals. Pneumonic plague can spread through coughing, but only if you are very close to the person. \"If you cough, you can spread it to another person, but that doesn't travel very far,\" said Dr. Glenn Wortmann. Recently, a worker at a plague research institute in Siberia died of pneumonia, and the institute was quarantined. The good news is that plague can be cured with antibiotics if you see a doctor early. There is a vaccine, but the WHO only recommends it for high-risk people like lab workers. There is no plague vaccine in the US. As Simon Clarke from the University of Reading said, \"People think the plague is gone... It's not. It's here amongst us.\"", "vocab": [{"word": "plague", "pos": "noun", "zh": "鼠疫", "note": "一种严重的传染病，历史上曾造成大量死亡。"}, {"word": "bacteria", "pos": "noun", "zh": "细菌", "note": "复数形式，单数是 bacterium。"}, {"word": "infection", "pos": "noun", "zh": "感染", "note": "常与 bacterial 或 viral 搭配。"}, {"word": "antibiotics", "pos": "noun", "zh": "抗生素", "note": "用于治疗细菌感染的药物。"}, {"word": "rodents", "pos": "noun", "zh": "啮齿动物", "note": "如老鼠、松鼠等。"}, {"word": "fleas", "pos": "noun", "zh": "跳蚤", "note": "一种会吸血的小昆虫。"}, {"word": "symptoms", "pos": "noun", "zh": "症状", "note": "疾病的表现，如发烧、咳嗽。"}, {"word": "lymph nodes", "pos": "noun", "zh": "淋巴结", "note": "身体免疫系统的一部分，感染时会肿痛。"}, {"word": "quarantined", "pos": "verb", "zh": "被隔离", "note": "过去分词，表示被隔离以防止疾病传播。"}, {"word": "vaccine", "pos": "noun", "zh": "疫苗", "note": "用于预防疾病的注射剂。"}], "title_original": "The plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year", "summary_original": "The plague, the rare bacterial infection infamously known for killing tens of millions in 14th-century Europe, never went away. It is now easily treated with antibiotics.", "body_original": "The plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year\nby The Associated Press\nedited by\nAndrew Zinin\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\nreputable news agency\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nThe plague, the rare bacterial infection infamously known for killing tens of millions in 14th-century Europe, never went away. It is now easily treated with antibiotics.\nSince the 1990s, most human cases have been reported in Africa, especially Congo and Madagascar, according to the\nWorld Health Organization\n.\nThere are an average of about seven human cases per year in the U.S.,\naccording to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\n, mostly in rural areas of the American West.\nA worker at a plague research institute in Siberia recently died of pneumonia.\nThe case has raised alarm\nin Russia after unconfirmed media reports suggested she might have died of pneumonic plague. Russian authorities say testing showed no evidence of plague, but the research institute has been quarantined.\n\"People think the plague is gone, it was something that occurred 500 years ago. It's not. It's here amongst us,\" said Simon Clarke of the University of Reading in the U.K. \"Unless you get rid of those wild animals, which of course is not possible, then you're not going to get rid of the bacteria.\"\nSurprised to hear the plague is still around? Here's what to know.\nPlague can attack lymph nodes, lungs and blood\nPeople in Siberia have been dying of plague for at least 5,500 years. The earliest evidence of a form of the disease was\nfound in teeth from ancient hunter-gatherers\nnorth of the region's Lake Baikal.\nPneumonic plague and the more well-known bubonic plague—also called the Black Death—are caused by the same bacteria, called Yersinia pestis.\nThe bacteria spread naturally among rodents like prairie dogs and rats. Fleas that jump from infected animals and bite people can spread the disease.\nThe bubonic plague is the most common form of the infection. It causes painfully swollen lymph nodes that are most commonly found in the groin, armpit and neck, called buboes. Other symptoms include sudden high fever and chills, headaches, and pain in the abdomen, legs and arms, according to the Cleveland Clinic.\nThere are two other forms of the plague: pneumonic plague, which can be caused by inhaling the bacteria, infects the lungs, while septicemic plague is an infection in the bloodstream. Both are fatal if not treated early, according to the World Health Organization.\nBubonic plague will often develop into the other two forms of plague if untreated.\nPlague can spread through fleas or bodily fluids\nOther than bites from infected fleas, people can also get plague by touching infected bodily fluids, so health experts recommend taking extra care when handling dead or sick animals.\nThe plague can also spread through the respiratory droplets of someone who has pneumonic plague.\n\"If you cough, you can spread it to another person, but that doesn't travel very far,\" said Dr. Glenn Wortmann, MedStar Washington Hospital Center. \"So you really need to be in face-to-face contact with that person to get pneumonic plague.\"\nThat's why \"the chance of this spreading widely in Russia and then extending here to the U.S. will not happen,\" he said.\nAntibiotics can treat plague\nThe plague was never eradicated, but we've gotten better at preventing its spread and treating it in humans.\nWhen treated early with antibiotics, the plague can be cured. The key is to get to a doctor fast—otherwise the plague can be deadly.\nThere is a vaccine, but the WHO only recommends it for people who are at high risk of infection, like laboratory and healthcare workers. There isn't strong evidence that vaccines can work against the pneumonic form.\nThere's no plague vaccine available in the U.S.\nKey medical concepts\nBubonic Plague\nPneumonic Plague\nYersinia pestis\nAntibiotics\nClinical categories\nInfectious diseases\nCommon illnesses & Prevention\nWho's behind this story?\nAndrew Zinin\nMaster's in physics with research experience. Long-time science news enthusiast. Plays key role in Science X's editorial success.\nFull profile →\n© 2026 The Associated Press. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten or redistributed without permission.\nCitation\n:\nThe plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year (2026, October 8)\nretrieved 8 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-plague-cases-africa-year.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T09:31:25.521123", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "The", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.275, "sm": 100, "em": 275, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "plague", "s": 0.287, "e": 0.637, "sm": 287, "em": 637, "cs": 4, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "is", "s": 0.65, "e": 0.75, "sm": 650, "em": 750, "cs": 